WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:05.000
Lesson 3.5 · Structure, Governance & Accountability
Roles and role descriptions

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.800
A role card shows the results expected and what each person can decide, founders included.

3
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:24.800
A role card describes what a person contributes, the results expected and what they can decide. A task list alone does not say what success looks like.

4
00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:25.800
What a role card holds

5
00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:38.611
Purpose and results: Why the role exists and what success looks like by the end of the year. For example: Bakery sales lead: two cafés and one office canteen buying bread every week by December.

6
00:00:38.611 --> 00:00:49.941
Responsibilities: The main areas of work the role owns, with one owner per area. For example: The baking lead owns the daily bake plan and the trainees' shift rota.

7
00:00:49.941 --> 00:01:02.011
Decisions and limits: What the role can decide alone, its spending limits and when to escalate. For example: The baking lead buys ingredients within the monthly budget; anything more goes to both founders.

8
00:01:02.011 --> 00:01:14.541
Links and support: Who the role works with and the support it needs to succeed. For example: The trainee support lead works with the youth centre and has a monthly check-in.

9
00:01:14.941 --> 00:01:15.541
Why they matter

10
00:01:15.541 --> 00:01:21.585
1. Co-founder roles are the first to blur; writing them down prevents friction.

11
00:01:21.585 --> 00:01:26.889
2. People know what they can decide without asking every time.

12
00:01:26.889 --> 00:01:33.193
3. One owner per area means nothing important falls between people.

13
00:01:33.593 --> 00:01:34.193
What they guide

14
00:01:34.193 --> 00:01:39.126
1. Your first job adverts and contracts in Module 4.

15
00:01:39.126 --> 00:01:44.430
2. Your RACI and delegation of authority, later in this module.

16
00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:50.363
3. Your check-ins: progress reviewed against each role's expected results.

17
00:01:50.763 --> 00:01:51.363
What to avoid

18
00:01:51.363 --> 00:01:56.667
Writing only a task list, with no results or authority.

19
00:01:56.667 --> 00:02:01.970
Shared ownership of an area, which usually means no ownership.

20
00:02:01.970 --> 00:02:07.904
Forgetting role cards for founders, volunteers and board officers.

21
00:02:08.504 --> 00:02:10.304
You write role cards for the founders and your first essential roles.

22
00:02:10.304 --> 00:02:14.304
Then save it, and download it as a PDF.

23
00:02:15.804 --> 00:02:18.904
Method into Impact
Better foundations. Stronger organisations.
